=========================================================
OOP PRINCIPLES – INTERVIEW NOTES (COPYABLE)
=========================================================

---------------------------------------------------------
1. ENCAPSULATION
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition
----------
Encapsulation is the process of bundling data (variables) and methods (functions) together inside a class while restricting direct access to the internal data.

It is achieved using:
• private
• protected
• public access specifiers

Purpose
-------
• Protect object state
• Prevent invalid data modification
• Hide implementation details
• Improve maintainability
• Improve security

Example
-------

class BankAccount
{
private:
    double balance;

public:
    BankAccount(double b): balance(b){}

    void deposit(double amount)
    {
        if(amount > 0)
            balance += amount;
    }

    double getBalance() const
    {
        return balance;
    }
};

Cannot access directly

account.balance = 1000;      // Error

Must use

account.deposit(1000);

Real-world Example
------------------
ATM

• Balance is hidden
• User cannot directly modify balance
• Deposit(), Withdraw(), CheckBalance() are exposed

Interview Answer
----------------
"Encapsulation means wrapping data and methods together into a class while restricting direct access to internal data. It protects object integrity by exposing only controlled interfaces."



---------------------------------------------------------
2. ABSTRACTION
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition
----------
Abstraction means showing only the essential features while hiding implementation details.

The user knows WHAT the object does,
not HOW it does it.

Achieved Using
--------------
• Abstract classes
• Pure virtual functions
• Interfaces

Example
-------

class Shape
{
public:
    virtual void draw() = 0;
};

class Circle : public Shape
{
public:
    void draw() override
    {
        cout << "Drawing Circle";
    }
};

User simply calls

shape->draw();

without knowing the implementation.

Real-world Examples
-------------------

• ATM
• Car steering wheel
• TV Remote
• Coffee Machine
• Mobile Camera

Interview Answer
----------------

"Abstraction hides implementation complexity while exposing only essential functionality. In C++, it is commonly achieved using abstract classes and pure virtual functions."



---------------------------------------------------------
ENCAPSULATION vs ABSTRACTION
---------------------------------------------------------

Encapsulation
-------------
• Hides data
• Uses access specifiers
• Focuses on security
• Protects object state

Abstraction
-----------
• Hides implementation
• Uses abstract classes/interfaces
• Focuses on simplicity
• Reduces complexity



---------------------------------------------------------
3. INHERITANCE
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition
----------

Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and behavior of another class.

Represents an

IS-A relationship

Example
-------

class Animal
{
public:
    void eat()
    {
        cout<<"Eating";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    void bark()
    {
        cout<<"Barking";
    }
};

Dog inherits eat() from Animal.

Types of Inheritance
--------------------

• Single
• Multiple
• Multilevel
• Hierarchical
• Hybrid

Advantages
----------

• Code reuse
• Extensibility
• Supports polymorphism
• Less duplication

Disadvantages
-------------

• Tight coupling
• Fragile base class problem
• Deep hierarchies become difficult to maintain

Interview Answer
----------------

"Inheritance allows a derived class to reuse and extend the functionality of a base class. It represents an IS-A relationship and enables runtime polymorphism."



---------------------------------------------------------
4. POLYMORPHISM
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition
----------

Polymorphism means

One Interface
Multiple Implementations

The same function behaves differently for different objects.

=========================================================
A. COMPILE-TIME POLYMORPHISM
=========================================================

Also called

• Static Polymorphism
• Early Binding

Achieved Using

• Function Overloading
• Operator Overloading
• Templates

Example

class Math
{
public:

    int add(int a,int b)
    {
        return a+b;
    }

    double add(double a,double b)
    {
        return a+b;
    }
};

Compiler decides which function to call.

Operator Overloading Example

Complex operator+(const Complex& rhs);

Template Example

template<typename T>
T maximum(T a,T b)
{
    return a>b ? a:b;
}

Advantages

• Faster
• No runtime overhead
• Resolved by compiler



=========================================================
B. RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
=========================================================

Also called

• Dynamic Polymorphism
• Late Binding

Achieved Using

• Virtual functions
• Function overriding

Example

class Animal
{
public:

    virtual void sound()
    {
        cout<<"Animal";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:

    void sound() override
    {
        cout<<"Dog";
    }
};

Animal* ptr = new Dog();

ptr->sound();

Output

Dog

Compiler decides function at runtime.



---------------------------------------------------------
HOW RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM WORKS
---------------------------------------------------------

Whenever a class has a virtual function

↓

Compiler creates

Virtual Table (vtable)

↓

Each object stores

Virtual Pointer (vptr)

↓

vptr points to class's vtable

↓

Calling

basePtr->virtualFunction();

↓

vptr looks into vtable

↓

Correct overridden function executes

This process is called

Dynamic Dispatch



Interview Answer
----------------

"Runtime polymorphism is implemented using virtual functions. The compiler creates a virtual table (vtable), and every object stores a hidden virtual pointer (vptr). During execution, the vptr is used to locate the correct overridden function."



---------------------------------------------------------
COMPILE-TIME vs RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
---------------------------------------------------------

Compile Time

• Early binding
• Faster
• Function overloading
• Operator overloading
• Templates

Runtime

• Late binding
• Slight runtime overhead
• Virtual functions
• Function overriding



---------------------------------------------------------
5. COMPOSITION vs INHERITANCE
---------------------------------------------------------

Inheritance

Represents

IS-A relationship

Example

Dog IS-A Animal

class Animal{};

class Dog : public Animal {};



Composition

Represents

HAS-A relationship

Example

Car HAS-A Engine

class Engine
{
};

class Car
{
private:
    Engine engine;
};



---------------------------------------------------------
WHY PREFER COMPOSITION OVER INHERITANCE?
---------------------------------------------------------

Advantages

• Low coupling
• High flexibility
• Easier maintenance
• Better testability
• Replace components independently
• Avoid fragile base class problem
• Supports runtime behavior changes

Example

Instead of

class FlyingCar : public Car {};

Use

class Engine{};
class Navigation{};
class MusicSystem{};

class Car
{
private:

    Engine engine;
    Navigation navigation;
    MusicSystem music;
};

Each component can be replaced independently.



---------------------------------------------------------
COMPOSITION vs INHERITANCE
---------------------------------------------------------

Composition

• HAS-A relationship
• Low coupling
• Highly flexible
• Runtime composition
• Preferred in modern software design

Inheritance

• IS-A relationship
• Tight coupling
• Less flexible
• Fixed hierarchy
• Use only when there is a true IS-A relationship



